@techreport{Bard,
abstract = {what is 22RIA120? What is pulse transformer? Show drafts A pulse transformer is a specialized type of transformer designed to efficiently transmit electric pulses between its windings and into the connected load. Unlike regular transformers that handle continuous waveforms, pulse transformers excel at dealing with short bursts of voltage or current. Here's a breakdown of their key features: Function: • Efficient pulse transmission: They faithfully maintain the shape and timing of the input pulse while potentially changing its voltage or current level. • Isolation: Many pulse transformers provide galvanic isolation between the primary and secondary circuits, preventing unwanted current flow and ground loops. This is crucial for safety and noise reduction in various applications. • Impedance matching: They can adjust the impedance (resistance) seen by the load, ensuring optimal power transfer and signal integrity. Applications: • Signal coupling: Connecting circuits with different voltage or current levels while maintaining isolation. • Gate drive transformers: Delivering control signals to power electronic switches like transistors in high-power applications. • Trigger transformers: Generating high-voltage pulses for ignition systems in lasers, flash lamps, and other equipment. • Power transfer in switched-mode power supplies (SMPS): Efficiently transferring energy in short bursts for devices like LED drivers and chargers. Key differences from regular transformers: • Pulse vs. continuous: Optimized for handling short, sharp pulses instead of constant waveforms.},
author = {Bard, Google},
file = {:C\:/Users/Mhdella/AppData/Local/Mendeley Ltd./Mendeley Desktop/Downloaded/Bard - Unknown - in 2024(2).pdf:pdf},
title = {in 2024}
}
