@book{Rawlins2011,
abstract = {The Theory That Would Not Die: How Bayes' Rule Cracked the Enigma Code, Hunted Down Russian Submarines, and Emerged Triumphant from Two Centuries of Controversy Sharon Bertsch McGrayne Yale University Press, 2011 - Sadly, Bayesian approaches are still used too infrequently by biostatisticians, despite the efforts of such luminaries as David Spiegelhalter and Deborah Ashby. McGrayne partly attributes this to the myopic views of the US Food and Drug Administration in relation to studies of pharmaceutical products. She does acknowledge, though, that drug companies use Bayesian approaches in their submissions for reimbursement by the English National Health Service by which (I presume) she is referring to the National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence. The Theory That Would Not Die is a book of scientific history rather than pure science but is none the worse for that. Readers will be amazed at the impact that Bayes' rule has had in diverse fields, as well as by its rejection by too many statisticians. One Bayesian biostatistician has told me that it is generational: and that statisticians who were taught, as youths, to use log books and slide rules just cannot do Bayesian analyses. I belong to this generation but reading McGrayne's book has made me determined to try, once again, to master the intricacies of Bayesian statistics.},
