@techreport{Ojadi2021a,
abstract = {At the peak of globalization, the increased geographical dispersion of value chain members has translated into larger volumes of cargo movement over greater distances by sea, air and land. This development has necessitated the need to engage freight forwarders in facilitating the movement and flow of cargo to the different destinations. While many countries have seized the opportunity to insert themselves into the different value chains of products by integrating freight forwarding activities to enhance trade, most of the sub-Saharan African countries are yet to do so. This study was aimed at identifying the barriers that impede the development of freight forwarding in Nigeria using the exploratory factor analysis methodology. The findings suggest that for freight forwarding to develop and play a significant role in linking members of the global value chain, it must address the lack of professionalism and raise the standards of practice and collaboration across the maritime industry.},
