@techreport{Shukla,
abstract = {Panel Abstract The CEII Act prevents utility companies from sharing customer data such as smart meter data from households, industrial consumption data to any third party. While modern machine learning models can learn power system behavior, their abilities are restricted by the quality of data provided. This calls for a serious need for developing high-quality synthetic datasets that can capture grid-scale transmission and storage fingerprints. Recent increase in storage deployment indicates an increased role that AI can play in generating rare event scenarios such as failure of storage units using technologies such as deep learning. This panel involves participants from academia, industry and national labs to contribute to architectural and implementational ideas that will lead to development of datasets ensuring fidelity to CEII act and quality of resulting machine learning solutions.},
